<p>Reports
  <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/current/javadoc-api/org/springframework/transaction/event/TransactionalEventListener.html">@TransactionalEventListener</a>
  methods that are also annotated with a plain
  <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/current/javadoc-api/org/springframework/transaction/annotation/Transactional.html">@Transactional</a>.</p>

<p>A transactional event listener runs during the cleanup phase of the original transaction, which is still active but in
  an undefined state. A regular <code>@Transactional</code> therefore executes in an undefined transactional state and
  fails at runtime. The combination is valid only when the propagation is <code>REQUIRES_NEW</code> or
  <code>NOT_SUPPORTED</code>, or when the method is also annotated with <code>@Async</code> (which runs the listener on a
  separate thread with a clean transactional slate).</p>

<p><b>Example:</b></p>
<pre><code lang="java">
  @Component
  class MyListener {
    @Transactional // reports: runs in an undefined transactional state
    @TransactionalEventListener
    void on(MyEvent event) { ... }
  }
</code></pre>

<p>After the quick fix:</p>
<pre><code lang="java">
  @Component
  class MyListener {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    @TransactionalEventListener
    void on(MyEvent event) { ... }
  }
</code></pre>

